00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:20.130
She never missed a beat.

1056
00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:22.870
She sang where her keys were.

1057
00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:24.410
It was amazing.

1058
00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:28.330
I did a mini deep dive on Son of a Preacher Man.

1059
00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:29.230
Did you really?

1060
00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:29.810
Yes.

1061
00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:31.510
I think we should take a break.

1062
00:39:31.510 --> 00:39:33.750
And when we come back, I'll tell you what I found.

1063
00:39:33.970 --> 00:39:34.230
Okay.

1064
00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:45.540
All right.

1065
00:39:45.660 --> 00:39:46.460
We are back.

1066
00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:51.820
And I'm going to start us off with a fan question from Yvette M and Alexandra S.

1067
00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:56.860
Why was the song Son of a Preacher Man chosen for Jan to sing to her baby?

1068
00:39:57.120 --> 00:39:59.380
I asked Aaron who picked the song.

1069
00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:05.480
And Aaron said he isn't sure if Mindy picked it per se, but she was pushing it.

1070
00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:08.840
He said, I don't remember how exactly it came up in the writer's room.

1071
00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:12.280
But the minute it was out there, Mindy was like, that's got to be it.

1072
00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:13.720
That has got to be it.

1073
00:40:14.180 --> 00:40:16.560
So I think we have Mindy to thank for that.

1074
00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:20.120
I got curious about the song, so I did a little deep dive on it.

1075
00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:24.800
Son of a Preacher Man was written by John Hurley and Ronnie Wilkins.

1076
00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:28.840
It was recorded by Dusty Springfield in 1968.

1077
00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:31.620
And her version is most popular.

1078
00:40:31.620 --> 00:40:34.080
It's been covered many times by many artists.

1079
00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:36.180
Here's what I found interesting.

1080
00:40:36.800 --> 00:40:42.620
Apparently, the rights to record the song were originally offered to Aretha Franklin.

1081
00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:44.940
She was the daughter of a preacher.

1082
00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:50.940
And the rumor is that she turned it down because she worried that maybe it might be a little disrespectful.

1083
00:40:51.280 --> 00:40:52.620
But she changed her mind.

1084
00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:53.400
No.

1085
00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:59.500
And she recorded a cover version of the song just one year later in 1969 for her album Soul Sister.

1086
00:40:59.860 --> 00:41:01.380
I had to hear it.

1087
00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:02.940
Sam, will you play it?

1088
00:41:34.040 --> 00:41:35.660
It's so good, isn't it?

1089
00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:36.100
Mm-hmm.

1090
00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:37.740
She always gives me chills.

1091
00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:41.560
Dolly Parton also did a cover in 1996 for her album Treasures.

1092
00:41:41.680 --> 00:41:43.360
And it is also a gem.

1093
00:41:43.540 --> 00:41:44.460
Do you want to hear that one?

1094
00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:45.100
Yes.

1095
00:41:46.900 --> 00:41:49.000
Billy Ray was a preacher's son.

1096
00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:51.540
And when his daddy would visit, he'd come along.

1097
00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:57.300
When they gathered around and started talking, that's when Billy would take me walking.

1098
00:41:57.740 --> 00:42:00.100
Through the backyard we'd go walking.

1099
00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:02.720
Billy looked into my eyes.

1100
00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:12.550
I mean, just—

1101
00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:14.550
I can listen to these women sing anything.

1102
00:42:14.690 --> 00:42:15.630
They're legends.

1103
00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:18.170
Legends in our podcast today.

1104
00:42:18.490 --> 00:42:20.210
Do we need to hear Dusty's?

1105
00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:22.470
I think we need to hear Dusty's.

1106
00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:24.130
I mean, we have to hear Dusty's.

1107
00:42:24.130 --> 00:42:26.250
It's Son of a Preacher Man Day today.

1108
00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:28.310
I mean, I just want to hear all of them.

1109
00:42:31.090 --> 00:42:35.890
I feel so embarrassed that I even attempted to sing it at all to recreate the scene with Ryan.

1110
00:42:36.170 --> 00:42:37.290
I am so sorry.

1111
00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:04.790
Oh, so good.

1112
00:43:04.990 --> 00:43:05.250
All right.

1113
00:43:05.350 --> 00:43:05.650
All right.

1114
00:43:05.650 --> 00:43:07.190
Here's what I have to say.

1115
00:43:07.430 --> 00:43:09.770
Kudos to John Hurley and Ronnie Wilkins,

1116
00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:17.790
because when you write a song that can be sung by all of these different people with their different spins on it and their different arrangements—

1117
00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:20.550
And it still knocks your socks off every time.

1118
00:43:20.650 --> 00:43:21.870
You have written a good song.

1119
00:43:22.010 --> 00:43:22.150
Yep.

1120
00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:30.190
One last thing I want to say about Son of a Preacher Man is that when Melora sang it, she improvised the snapping.

1121
00:43:30.730 --> 00:43:31.870
And you know what?

1122
00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:34.750
But it about got me on the floor.

1123
00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:41.770
We were so trying to suppress laughter that one more thing was like the tipping point, you know?

1124
00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:42.170
Yes.

1125
00:43:42.170 --> 00:43:48.030
And so as she's swaying and singing, when she just starts doing that snap, I was like, oh, Lord, I'm not going to make it.

1126
00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:49.150
I am not going to make it.

1127
00:43:49.470 --> 00:43:54.130
There are really great scripted talking heads of people reacting to it.

1128
00:43:54.250 --> 00:43:57.950
And Stan Lee had a great one where he was like, I used to like that song.

1129
00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:04.580
Well, Jim is going to try to describe it to Pam.

1130
00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:08.720
She's at the laundromat, and she can't hear anything he's saying.

1131
00:44:08.980 --> 00:44:11.320
Why couldn't she just walk away from the machines, Jenna?

1132
00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:12.080
You know what?

1133
00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:12.740
Megan P.

1134
00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:15.040
would like to know the answer to that question as well.

1135
00:44:15.200 --> 00:44:15.920
Megan, it's the first thing I thought.

1136
00:44:16.060 --> 00:44:17.220
She hasn't talked to him all day.

1137
00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:18.660
She finally has free time.

1138
00:44:18.880 --> 00:44:20.260
Walk away from the machine.

1139
00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:22.660
I'll tell you two things.

1140
00:44:22.900 --> 00:44:27.260
Number one, right before this phone call, there was a bit of the scene that got clipped.

1141
00:44:28.020 --> 00:44:34.440
Pam is with her friend at the laundromat, and they are together, and they're talking about a class assignment.

1142
00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:37.800
And she says, oh, just one second, I'm going to take this call.

1143
00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:41.140
So maybe she felt like she couldn't walk away.

1144
00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:50.360
But also in the script, there is a version where she does walk away from the machines, and the problem is not that the machines are loud.

1145
00:44:50.360 --> 00:44:56.160
The problem is simply that Pam is not understanding what's so bad about Jan singing to her baby.

1146
00:44:56.400 --> 00:44:58.840
So it's another kind of like...

1147
00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:00.060
Miscommunication.

1148
00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:02.400
Like, you had to be there kind of things.

1149
00:45:02.660 --> 00:45:06.040
But they kept the loud laundry machine version in.

1150
00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:14.640
I'll have you know, location alert, the laundromat was Launderland on Sherman Way in North Hollywood, California.

1151
00:45:15.080 --> 00:45:20.420
I like to throw these out there because there are these office tours where people go around and look at our locations.

1152
00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:23.940
And so I thought, I don't know if you want to see where Pam did her laundry.

1153
00:45:24.100 --> 00:45:24.540
That's where.

1154
00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:24.940
Okay.

1155
00:45:25.340 --> 00:45:29.920
So Pam has this talking head at the laundromat where she says that she's not frustrated.

1156
00:45:30.140 --> 00:45:33.920
You know, they have these kind of days where they don't connect, even when they're together in Scranton.

1157
00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:38.700
And then she discovers that she washed her lipstick, and it got all over her green towel.

1158
00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:43.720
You know, I recently washed Lee's wallet and car key.

1159
00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:45.180
I remember this.

1160
00:45:45.700 --> 00:45:51.660
You called me, and you're like, I washed the keys to the car, his whole entire wallet.

1161
00:45:51.720 --> 00:45:52.360
Whole wallet.

1162
00:45:52.480 --> 00:45:52.980
Whole wallet.

1163
00:45:53.060 --> 00:45:55.140
And now he couldn't find his wallet for days.

1164
00:45:55.480 --> 00:45:56.700
He was like, where is my wallet?

1165
00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:57.960
I can't find my wallet.

1166
00:45:58.260 --> 00:45:59.040
It was in the laundry basket?

1167
00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:00.740
It was in his pocket.

1168
00:46:00.920 --> 00:46:01.160
Yeah.

1169
00:46:01.360 --> 00:46:02.840
In the laundry basket.

1170
00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:04.920
And I didn't check the pockets.

1171
00:46:04.920 --> 00:46:06.360
I threw in the laundry.

1172
00:46:06.820 --> 00:46:08.580
I found it in the dryer.

1173
00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:12.340
It went through a full wash cycle, a full dryer cycle.

1174
00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:15.340
I said, babe, I found your wallet.

1175
00:46:16.900 --> 00:46:19.380
Wait, did the credit cards, did it all get melty?

1176
00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:21.020
Did he have to get everything new?

1177
00:46:21.260 --> 00:46:24.300
Lady, his vaccination card was in his wallet.

1178
00:46:24.460 --> 00:46:24.800
Oh, no.

1179
00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:26.280
All of his credit cards.

1180
00:46:26.700 --> 00:46:28.380
He was like, oh, my God.

1181
00:46:28.800 --> 00:46:30.380
The vaccination card made it.

1182
00:46:30.460 --> 00:46:31.880
It was a little frayed at the top.

1183
00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:32.980
He went out.

1184
00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:35.640
He bought some gas, and the credit cards worked.

1185
00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:37.160
Well, how about that?

1186
00:46:37.880 --> 00:46:38.620
So I don't know.

1187
00:46:38.620 --> 00:46:39.740
I guess you didn't have it.

1188
00:46:39.780 --> 00:46:41.000
You didn't have it on high heat.

1189
00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:42.920
No, I did dry on low.

1190
00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:48.760
And the car key still works, because it's one of those little like, yeah, it still works.

1191
00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:54.020
While all of the singing is going on, Dwight decides he's going to put the stroller to the

1192
00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:54.780
ultimate teSt.

1193
00:46:55.200 --> 00:46:55.560
Yes.

1194
00:46:55.800 --> 00:47:00.980
We had a fan question from Beth E., Rachel R., Amy F., and Julie G.

1195
00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:06.440
How many of those strollers did you use for the scenes where Dwight tests its durability?

1196
00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:10.640
This is why I was on the phone with Phil Schaefer an hour yesterday.

1197
00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:13.920
Well, how many did they use?

1198
00:47:14.260 --> 00:47:15.300
I'll tell you.

1199
00:47:15.520 --> 00:47:21.280
It started with Phil Schaefer doing a ton of research to find the perfect stroller for

1200
00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:21.960
this episode.

1201
00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:29.360
He said he and Greg had a long talk about how Jan was the kind of person who would have

1202
00:47:29.360 --> 00:47:30.860
to have the fanciest stroller.

1203
00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:38.120
In the script, it's written that the stroller is indestructible, and he wanted to find something

1204
00:47:38.120 --> 00:47:40.820
that would even look out of place in Scranton.

1205
00:47:41.080 --> 00:47:48.260
So he went on a search for an indestructible luxury stroller, and he found out about the

1206
00:47:48.260 --> 00:47:52.580
Orbit baby stroller, which was kind of a startup stroller at the time.

1207
00:47:53.240 --> 00:47:58.660
It launched in September of 2006, so just two years before this episode.

1208
00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:02.800
It was invented by two dads, Brian White and Joseph High.

1209
00:48:03.060 --> 00:48:04.040
I think it's High.

1210
00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:05.980
Joseph, I'm sorry if I got that wrong.

1211
00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:12.780
And they made it because Brian had just become a dad, and he said that his back really hurt

1212
00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:18.000
whenever he was trying to get the baby in and out of the car seat and the car.

1213
00:48:18.000 --> 00:48:22.460
So in his garage, they made a wooden prototype.

1214
00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:28.120
They studied all kinds of things, but they also sought to use green materials.

1215
00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:29.280
I like these fellas.

1216
00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:34.520
Yeah, and this car seat was cool because Phil said it looked kind of weird, but it also

1217
00:48:34.520 --> 00:48:36.860
rotates 360 degrees.

1218
00:48:37.620 --> 00:48:39.600
So Phil was like, it was so cool.

1219
00:48:39.700 --> 00:48:44.360
Like, if you want to get your baby out of the car and not remove the car seat, you can swivel

1220
00:48:44.360 --> 00:48:47.380
it, and then you can get your baby out of the car really easy.

1221
00:48:47.380 --> 00:48:52.500
Well, Jenna, you know, Phil Shea had a few extra Orbit's left over, and I was a new mom

1222
00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:55.500
on set, and he always was looking out for us.

1223
00:48:55.600 --> 00:48:58.440
And he said, Ange, do you want one of these Orbit strollers?

1224
00:48:58.540 --> 00:48:59.420
They're pretty amazing.

1225
00:48:59.660 --> 00:49:00.720
And I said, yeah.

1226
00:49:01.320 --> 00:49:04.320
And that was my favorite thing about it, too, what you just said.

1227
00:49:04.800 --> 00:49:07.500
You could pivot, and it would lock into place.

1228
00:49:07.600 --> 00:49:12.740
So if you swiveled it, it could face left, right, front, back, and it would lock into place.

1229
00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:17.360
Not only was it great when you got your baby out of the car seat, what I loved is you

1230
00:49:17.360 --> 00:49:21.640
could attach it to the stroller, and let's say you're on a walk, and the sun is kind of

1231
00:49:21.640 --> 00:49:25.440
beating down, and you don't want to put the little flap over your baby because your baby

1232
00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:26.940
wants to be able to see where you're going.

1233
00:49:27.340 --> 00:49:30.600
You could just pivot, so then the baby could look at everything on the right side of the

1234
00:49:30.600 --> 00:49:33.620
street, or everything on the left side, or look at you.

1235
00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:34.600
It was so great.

1236
00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:39.080
I love that Phil gave you a stroller, and that he gave you a chair from WorkBus.

1237
00:49:39.400 --> 00:49:39.860
I know.

1238
00:49:39.860 --> 00:49:41.160
Do you have any more things?

1239
00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:43.200
I'm sure I do.

1240
00:49:45.140 --> 00:49:47.580
Maybe your memory will be jogged as we go along.

1241
00:49:47.600 --> 00:49:49.060
As we go through the episodes.

1242
00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:54.900
Well, Phil said that he called Brian on the phone, and he was like, listen, I've researched

1243
00:49:54.900 --> 00:49:58.460
your stroller, and I want to put it on the television show The Office.

1244
00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:04.180
And he told him the storyline, and Brian agreed to send us 10 strollers.

1245
00:50:04.180 --> 00:50:07.860
And then Brian flew out and watched the taping.

1246
00:50:08.200 --> 00:50:12.780
Phil also said the stroller really was indestructible.

1247
00:50:13.100 --> 00:50:14.220
He's not kidding.

1248
00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:20.360
There are so many deleted scenes of Rain as Dwight doing things to that stroller, and the

1249
00:50:20.360 --> 00:50:22.020
stroller just keeps on trekking.

1250
00:50:22.140 --> 00:50:23.040
There's way more.

1251
00:50:23.840 --> 00:50:28.460
Phil told me that one of his favorite things to do was to reach out to these smaller companies

1252
00:50:28.460 --> 00:50:34.540
that didn't normally do national advertising and feature their products on the show or

1253
00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:35.440
collaborate with them.

1254
00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:41.720
And he said another one of the reasons that you saw all of the local foods or the local

1255
00:50:41.720 --> 00:50:48.460
sodas on The Office was because one of our characters is eating like Lay's potato chips.

1256
00:50:48.760 --> 00:50:54.380
That could be a conflict with maybe another big national potato chip brand that would have

1257
00:50:54.380 --> 00:50:57.340
a national commercial running during our show, so we weren't allowed.

1258
00:50:57.340 --> 00:51:02.840
So his choice was he could tape over Lay's or create a fakie bag.

1259
00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:03.600
Fakie chip bag.

1260
00:51:03.800 --> 00:51:04.040
Right.

1261
00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:10.780
He said, but what he liked to do was feature products, local products like a Hearst chip.

1262
00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:12.020
A smaller company.

1263
00:51:12.280 --> 00:51:16.420
That is not going to be in competition for these national spots.

1264
00:51:16.680 --> 00:51:19.180
And that's another way that he focused in on The Orbit.

1265
00:51:19.300 --> 00:51:21.820
He would try to find these companies that could be approved.

1266
00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:25.500
Well, I love that because, you know, we love supporting small business.

1267
00:51:25.880 --> 00:51:26.120
Yeah.

1268
00:51:26.120 --> 00:51:30.220
Well, The Orbit stroller is still available, and it is now a big business.

1269
00:51:30.700 --> 00:51:33.080
I saw online you can get one at Nordstrom.

1270
00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:35.180
Well, Jan is done singing.

1271
00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:36.300
She's pooped out.

1272
00:51:36.520 --> 00:51:38.740
I mean, all that singing is going to tire you out.

1273
00:51:38.820 --> 00:51:39.040
Yeah.

1274
00:51:39.180 --> 00:51:40.400
She wants to take a nap.

1275
00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:42.260
She gives the baby to Michael.

1276
00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:48.300
And she says, when I wake up, I want to talk to you about Astrid's 529, which I looked

1277
00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:48.460
up.

1278
00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:50.520
It's like a savings plan for college.

1279
00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:50.980
Yeah.

1280
00:51:50.980 --> 00:51:54.760
So what, Michael has to go to all the birthing classes, but he doesn't get to be there for

1281
00:51:54.760 --> 00:51:55.560
the birth of the baby?

1282
00:51:55.680 --> 00:51:57.800
And now he's going to contribute to the college fund?

1283
00:51:57.900 --> 00:51:59.020
That's what it sounds like.

1284
00:51:59.180 --> 00:52:00.680
Jan needs to go buh-bye.

1285
00:52:01.020 --> 00:52:01.680
I agree.

1286
00:52:02.080 --> 00:52:05.120
Well, Michael only holds the baby for a short time.

1287
00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:09.280
He's not feeling that connection, and he gives the baby to Phyllis.

1288
00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:09.780
Yeah.

1289
00:52:09.780 --> 00:52:11.920
First, Michael has to go to the warehouse.

1290
00:52:12.320 --> 00:52:14.400
He needs some advice from Daryl.

1291
00:52:15.140 --> 00:52:18.080
We had a fan question from Ethan B.

1292
00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:23.000
Did Steve improvise the moment when he asks if he can hold Daryl?

1293
00:52:23.980 --> 00:52:28.440
They get on this runner where Daryl's like, do you want to hold this?

1294
00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:30.720
Do you want to hold that and see if you have a connection?

1295
00:52:30.860 --> 00:52:31.900
Do you want to hold me?

1296
00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:34.240
No, you guys, that was in the script.

1297
00:52:34.900 --> 00:52:37.460
That whole scene was scripted just like that.

1298
00:52:37.460 --> 00:52:40.420
Well, Jan is still sleeping in the deleted scenes.

1299
00:52:40.600 --> 00:52:43.260
She's like snoring, and everyone's looking at her.

1300
00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:46.540
She's covered herself with a plastic tablecloth from the shower,

1301
00:52:47.460 --> 00:52:51.580
and she wakes up and has a pretty amazing exchange with Holly.

1302
00:52:51.800 --> 00:52:55.140
Where she realizes what we have realized,

1303
00:52:55.220 --> 00:52:57.880
which is that Holly has a little bit of Michael Scott in her.

1304
00:52:58.120 --> 00:52:58.480
Yeah.

1305
00:52:58.640 --> 00:53:00.780
She's making some real Michael Scott jokes here.

1306
00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:03.200
Yeah, and Jan is like, oh, I get it.

1307
00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:03.740
Funny.

1308
00:53:05.920 --> 00:53:09.120
But Astrid is now with Angela and Andy.

1309
00:53:09.300 --> 00:53:10.480
I love this scene so much.

1310
00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:15.060
Angela, please tell me everything about putting all these vegetables on this baby.

1311
00:53:15.460 --> 00:53:15.760
All right.

1312
00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:18.300
They had the table completely laid out.

1313
00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:20.180
They had it all staged.

1314
00:53:20.400 --> 00:53:23.560
They had the mom with the baby in the wings standing right there.

1315
00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:27.240
And literally, right before action, the mom put the baby down,

1316
00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:32.560
and then I just put that big, giant, fake piece of lettuce on the baby.

1317
00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:36.520
It was really hard for me because I was a new mom, and I just was like, okay,

1318
00:53:36.600 --> 00:53:38.060
I just want to do this so faSt.

1319
00:53:38.160 --> 00:53:39.380
I want to do the scene so quickly.

1320
00:53:39.700 --> 00:53:44.040
And then it was so hard for me as a mom to not really connect with the baby at all

1321
00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:45.860
and just say like, I need it to look up here.

1322
00:53:46.060 --> 00:53:46.980
Make it look up here.

1323
00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:47.440
Yes.

1324
00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:49.320
But it's such a window into Angela.

1325
00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:50.940
You know, she was a pageant baby.

1326
00:53:51.180 --> 00:53:52.740
She dresses up her cats.

1327
00:53:52.960 --> 00:53:53.260
Yes.

1328
00:53:53.460 --> 00:53:57.740
I feel like her kids are going to be dressed up a lot for all kinds of photos.

1329
00:53:57.780 --> 00:53:59.300
And Andy is on board.

1330
00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:07.440
I always imagined that Angela dressed Philip in like the little knee socks and shorts and

1331
00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:12.040
suspenders with the little Peter Pan collar that you always see little Prince George wearing.

1332
00:54:12.580 --> 00:54:14.580
That's how I imagined Philip dresses.

1333
00:54:14.940 --> 00:54:15.520
Oh, yeah.

1334
00:54:15.700 --> 00:54:19.520
And I also imagined that Angela had like a little sailor suit for him.

1335
00:54:19.660 --> 00:54:19.980
For sure.

1336
00:54:20.180 --> 00:54:20.800
With a sailor hat.

1337
00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:21.800
A hundred percent.

1338
00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:22.740
All of that.

1339
00:54:22.940 --> 00:54:23.160
Yes.

1340
00:54:23.160 --> 00:54:29.120
Jan comes in as Andy and Angela are having their Ann Geddes moment, creating this baby

1341
00:54:29.120 --> 00:54:32.920
salad photo, and she is not happy.

1342
00:54:33.100 --> 00:54:36.180
No, she's irritated and she wants to go.

1343
00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:37.160
She's done.

1344
00:54:37.300 --> 00:54:37.900
She's done.

1345
00:54:38.080 --> 00:54:42.080
She finds Michael in the kitchen and she's ready to go.

1346
00:54:42.520 --> 00:54:44.960
And Michael insults Holly again.

1347
00:54:44.960 --> 00:54:49.640
I guess we've failed to mention that throughout this whole episode, every time Michael, Holly,

1348
00:54:49.780 --> 00:54:54.000
and Jan are together, Michael just keeps, you know, insulting her.

1349
00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:57.900
When Holly was going to go into the conference room, he's like, ugh, HR.

1350
00:54:58.160 --> 00:55:00.300
Now he says she smells like rotten tomatoes.

1351
00:55:00.620 --> 00:55:01.280
And dirt.

1352
00:55:01.600 --> 00:55:02.420
And dirt.

1353
00:55:02.620 --> 00:55:04.260
She smells like tomatoes and dirt.

1354
00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:04.940
Yeah.

1355
00:55:05.380 --> 00:55:06.460
And you know what?

1356
00:55:06.560 --> 00:55:07.840
Jan sees all of it.

1357
00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:09.580
Jan knows what Michael's doing.

1358
00:55:14.960 --> 00:55:15.200
She turns around.

1359
00:55:15.280 --> 00:55:17.080
She's like, there is one more thing you could do for me.

1360
00:55:17.500 --> 00:55:18.440
And he's like, what?

1361
00:55:18.620 --> 00:55:20.740
And she says, don't date Holly.

1362
00:55:21.160 --> 00:55:21.520
Yeah.

1363
00:55:21.800 --> 00:55:23.640
And he's like, why would I hate her?

1364
00:55:23.760 --> 00:55:24.260
Why would I?

1365
00:55:24.680 --> 00:55:25.200
You know?

1366
00:55:25.340 --> 00:55:27.020
And he clearly likes her.

1367
00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:27.940
Yeah.

1368
00:55:28.760 --> 00:55:29.420
Jan leaves.

1369
00:55:29.660 --> 00:55:32.180
She leaves the stroller that was gifted to her.

1370
00:55:32.380 --> 00:55:33.040
I know.

1371
00:55:33.380 --> 00:55:34.320
So tacky.

1372
00:55:35.220 --> 00:55:36.020
So tacky.

1373
00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:36.880
Tacky Jan.

1374
00:55:37.220 --> 00:55:39.380
What was the point of the baby shower?

1375
00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:43.920
That she could get a nap and maybe get some money out of Michael for a college fund.

1376
00:55:43.920 --> 00:55:45.020
That's what it was.

1377
00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:45.380
Yeah.

1378
00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:47.480
Well, Michael is going to go upstairs.

1379
00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:53.340
And he finds Holly and she says, are you going to still be mean to me?

1380
00:55:53.820 --> 00:55:55.580
And he just gives her a hug.

1381
00:55:55.760 --> 00:55:58.760
It's the warmest, most intimate hug.

1382
00:55:59.180 --> 00:56:00.360
It's so intimate.

1383
00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:02.420
Amy Ryan is so great.

1384
00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:04.560
Her eyes just fill with tears.

1385
00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:15.900
And then Michael has this talking head where he says he didn't feel much when he held Astrid, but he got a good feeling when he held Holly.

1386
00:56:16.280 --> 00:56:17.960
He asked her out on a date.

1387
00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:18.440
Mm-hmm.

1388
00:56:18.620 --> 00:56:20.660
A real official date.

1389
00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:22.060
And she says yes.

1390
00:56:22.340 --> 00:56:23.400
She sure does.

1391
00:56:23.400 --> 00:56:26.500
So we're ending with a little love connection there.

1392
00:56:27.200 --> 00:56:32.700
But now Jim and Pam are both trying to call each other at the same time and they miss each other.

1393
00:56:32.700 --> 00:56:35.040
They both go to voicemail at the same time.

1394
00:56:35.220 --> 00:56:38.620
And I remember Greg talking to me about this scene.

1395
00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:47.160
I just loved this idea that we're going to hear each side of their voicemail message and you're going to see they really are connected.

1396
00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:57.200
They both recall this same story about the time that, you know, some man came into the laundromat and Jim had to save Pam and then they bought a washer dryer.

1397
00:56:57.420 --> 00:56:59.320
They're recalling this same event.

1398
00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:03.760
And at the end, they both say, I guess this was just one of those days.

1399
00:57:03.780 --> 00:57:04.760
I miss you.

1400
00:57:04.900 --> 00:57:05.780
I wish you were home.

1401
00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:14.380
Aaron shared with me that he was just so curious how this scene would turn out because you guys filmed it in two different locations.

1402
00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.220
You didn't have the other person there.

1403
00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:18.660
No, I couldn't hear John.

1404
00:57:18.780 --> 00:57:20.560
It was like I was really leaving a message.

1405
00:57:20.800 --> 00:57:21.040
Yeah.

1406
00:57:21.240 --> 00:57:23.620
And so you guys were just in this vacuum separate.

1407
00:57:23.920 --> 00:57:27.720
And then in editing, they were going to find the moment and create it.

1408
00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:32.520
And he just sort of marveled at how the whole thing came together and just so perfectly.

1409
00:57:32.900 --> 00:57:33.260
Yes.

1410
00:57:33.460 --> 00:57:38.000
Well, I had to really imagine in my head as an actor that I was just leaving a message.

1411
00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:42.760
So in the script, I could see how our dialogue was going to alternate and sync up.

1412
00:57:42.760 --> 00:57:51.380
But I put that out of my mind and I just did my half without thinking of John's lines in my head at all.

1413
00:57:51.620 --> 00:57:51.720
Right.

1414
00:57:51.720 --> 00:57:53.320
And we filmed it like a monologue.

1415
00:57:53.920 --> 00:57:54.160
Yeah.

1416
00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:55.200
Well, I loved it.

1417
00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:56.560
I thought it was really sweet.

1418
00:57:56.560 --> 00:58:01.340
And it also made me a little sad because you can tell the distance is starting to take a toll.

1419
00:58:01.720 --> 00:58:01.940
Yeah.

1420
00:58:02.500 --> 00:58:04.180
Well, that ends the episode.

1421
00:58:04.580 --> 00:58:07.620
But Angela, I just realized I skipped a fan question.

1422
00:58:07.860 --> 00:58:08.160
Okay.

1423
00:58:08.400 --> 00:58:10.380
And I don't want to leave Evan L.

1424
00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:10.790
hanging.

1425
00:58:11.080 --> 00:58:11.760
Okay, Evan.

1426
00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:22.040
Evan wrote in to say at 17 minutes, 30 seconds, was that really rain driving around with the stroller tied to the back of the car or was that a stunt driver?

1427
00:58:22.460 --> 00:58:24.440
Well, we had a stunt driver.

1428
00:58:24.740 --> 00:58:26.900
His name was Sean Crowder.

1429
00:58:27.460 --> 00:58:30.440
We also had a stunt coordinator named Wally Crowder.

1430
00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:33.760
And so Sean did the driving parts.

1431
00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:39.420
Now, you know, there were some shots of rain inside the car, but the other stuff, that was a stunt driver.

1432
00:58:39.880 --> 00:58:40.240
Yes.

1433
00:58:40.360 --> 00:58:44.000
We would often do the driving that was just sort of like where you pull up and stop.

1434
00:58:44.200 --> 00:58:44.460
Yeah.

1435
00:58:44.460 --> 00:58:48.720
But then if there was like aggressive driving, that's when the stunt driver would take over.

1436
00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:49.100
Yes.

1437
00:58:49.500 --> 00:58:50.740
Well, I loved this episode.

1438
00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:54.520
I thought it was so good and layered with so much heartfelt stuff.

1439
00:58:54.520 --> 00:58:58.680
And before we say goodbye, I have a little bit of trivia for you.

1440
00:58:58.940 --> 00:58:59.200
Okay.

1441
00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:06.320
According to babycenter.com, here are the top girl and boy names in 2008.

1442
00:59:06.320 --> 00:59:08.380
Oh, from when we did this episode.

1443
00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:09.100
That's right.

1444
00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:10.820
Any guesses for the girl?

1445
00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:12.820
Emma.

1446
00:59:13.220 --> 00:59:13.720
Yes.

1447
00:59:13.820 --> 00:59:14.140
Ah!

1448
00:59:14.360 --> 00:59:15.220
You got it.

1449
00:59:15.420 --> 00:59:17.580
I was thinking maybe because of the friends baby.

1450
00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:18.440
Yes.

1451
00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:19.640
Rachel had Emma.

1452
00:59:20.000 --> 00:59:21.340
Really put that name on the map.

1453
00:59:21.760 --> 00:59:23.220
Guess what the boy name was.

1454
00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:28.100
I guessed Emma for the girl, like you, but I did not guess right for the boy name.

1455
00:59:29.040 --> 00:59:29.560
Liam.

1456
00:59:30.400 --> 00:59:30.800
Aiden.

1457
00:59:31.540 --> 00:59:32.060
Aiden.

1458
00:59:32.500 --> 00:59:34.400
Aiden was the top boy name.

1459
00:59:34.400 --> 00:59:35.860
Was that from Sex and the City?

1460
00:59:36.320 --> 00:59:37.300
I don't know.

1461
00:59:37.520 --> 00:59:38.500
I guessed Jack.

1462
00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:39.100
Okay.

1463
00:59:39.280 --> 00:59:40.660
He was in the top five.

1464
00:59:40.980 --> 00:59:43.140
But Aiden and Emma.

1465
00:59:43.840 --> 00:59:45.060
Well, there it is.

1466
00:59:45.180 --> 00:59:47.060
You've learned some new baby showers.

1467
00:59:47.300 --> 00:59:48.120
The Sip and See.

1468
00:59:48.500 --> 00:59:50.700
M&Ms are still printing names.

1469
00:59:51.040 --> 00:59:51.480
Yeah.

1470
00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:53.640
And the Orbit stroller is actually really good.

1471
00:59:53.920 --> 00:59:55.180
And Emma and Aiden.

1472
00:59:55.680 --> 00:59:56.620
2008 babies.

1473
00:59:56.800 --> 00:59:57.400
Where are you now?

1474
00:59:57.560 --> 00:59:59.960
I would love it if an Emma and Aiden got married.

1475
01:00:00.400 --> 01:00:00.880
Oh.

1476
01:00:01.720 --> 01:00:03.320
Well, that was Baby Shower.

1477
01:00:03.400 --> 01:00:04.460
We'll see you next week.

1478
01:00:04.620 --> 01:00:05.320
Bye, you guys.

1479
